Migrations for the engine's two tables (`proposals`, `audit_events`).

Run them from `apps/engine`, which is where `alembic.ini` lives:

    alembic upgrade head        apply everything
    alembic current             what revision this database is at
    alembic history --verbose   the full history
    alembic downgrade -1        undo the last migration

The database URL is NOT in `alembic.ini`. It comes from `DATABASE_URL` via `app.config.Settings`, so
these commands act on exactly the database the service would use:

    DATABASE_URL=postgresql+asyncpg://azmoth:azmoth@localhost:5432/azmoth alembic upgrade head

The container runs `alembic upgrade head` before uvicorn (see the Dockerfile's entrypoint), so a
deploy migrates itself. Local development does not need any of this: `DATABASE_AUTO_CREATE=true` is
the default and creates the tables directly — but note that a database built that way has no
revision stamp, so `alembic upgrade head` against it will try to create tables that already exist.
Delete the SQLite file, or `alembic stamp head`, before switching a dev database over.

`DATABASE_AUTO_CREATE` only applies to SQLite. `app/db/session.py::init_models` refuses it on a
Postgres URL in *every* environment, because that is how this went wrong once: see below.


## "relation X already exists"

`alembic upgrade head` failing on a table that is already there means the schema and
`alembic_version` disagree. Four different things cause that and they do not share a repair, so
find out which one it is before typing anything:

    python scripts/migrate.py --diagnose

It reflects the database and reports, per pending revision, which of the objects that revision
creates already exist. Two verdicts matter:

* **"everything it creates is already present"** — the revision has nothing left to do. Either it
  applied and the stamp was lost, or this schema was built by another deployment. Confirm the rows
  in those tables are the ones you expect, then `alembic stamp <revision>` and carry on.

* **"but still missing: …"** — the revision did *not* half-apply. A revision is a unit; half of it
  present means something else created those objects, without knowing the revision existed.
  **Do not stamp**: the missing half would never be created, and the schema would silently disagree
  with `app/db/models.py` until something read it. Run `python scripts/migrate.py` instead —
  `0010` is idempotent and fills in only what is absent.

If neither fits, the tables may belong to a different database than you think. `scripts/migrate.py`
logs the resolved URL with the password stripped as its first line; check it against the deployment
before doing anything else.

### The incident this section is written from

`organization_billing` already existed in the Neon database and `alembic_version` read
`0009_api_usage_logs`. A laptop had been pointed at the deployed `DATABASE_URL` with `APP_ENV` at
its default of `development`, where `DATABASE_AUTO_CREATE` defaults to true — so app startup ran
`Base.metadata.create_all` against production. `create_all` is `CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS` over the
whole of `app/db/models.py`: it skipped `api_usage_logs`, which already existed, created
`organization_billing` and `billing_invoices`, which did not, and never touched `alembic_version`,
which it has never heard of.

`alembic stamp head` was the obvious fix and would have been the wrong one — `0010` also adds
`api_usage_logs.invoices_processed`, which `create_all` could not add to a table it had skipped, and
that column is what an invoice is counted from. Stamping would have declared the database migrated
with the column still missing.

Three changes came out of it, and `tests/test_migration_recovery.py` holds each to its claim:
`init_models` now refuses `create_all` on any Postgres URL rather than only under
`APP_ENV=production`; `0010` creates only what is absent, so the databases already damaged can
migrate through it; and `--diagnose` tells the two repairs apart instead of leaving it to judgement
during an outage.

To add a migration after changing `app/db/models.py`:

    alembic revision --autogenerate -m "what changed"

Then READ the generated file. Autogenerate does not see a rename (it emits a drop plus an add,
which loses the data), and it cannot know that `audit_events` is append-only — a migration that
rewrites audit rows is a compliance event, not a schema change.
